# =====================================================================
# rb4011_fixsyslog_delta — HD-313: remote-syslog egress refresh-on-boot SAFETY NET
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Root-cause note (2026-09-08): the HD-313 "zero UDP/514" blocker on the RB4011 was
# NOT a RouterOS logging fault — it was the VPS-side WireGuard peer AllowedIPs missing
# the router's own wg-s2s address (`wg_s2s_vps.router_ip`; SSOT `wg_s2s_vps.allowed_ips`
# in group_vars/all.yml). WireGuard silently drops inner packets whose source IP is not
# in the peer's AllowedIPs, so every syslog packet (src-address=wg-s2s) died at the VPS
# tunnel endpoint. Fix = the SSOT list; verified end-to-end 2026-09-08.
#
# This script+scheduler is retained as harmless SELF-HEALING INSURANCE: RouterOS has no
# direct enable/disable for a logging action, so IF a future boot ever fails to (re)init
# the remote action, a startup scheduler re-applies the target after the tunnel is up.
# A `/system scheduler` entry (start-time=startup) runs this ONCE at boot (~3s), the
# script's own 10s delay covers wg-s2s/route bring-up, 1s more, then re-apply the target.
#
# Action-naming (HD-313 live rule): RouterOS 7 scheduler/script names are
# letters+numbers ONLY — `fix-syslog-egress` (hyphens) is rejected, so the
# names the script+scheduler `fixsyslog` (same reason the HD-313
# action became `centralsyslog`). The toggle targets `centralsyslog` (the actual
# HD-313 remote action on the RB4011, live-confirmed) — NOT the bundled
# default `remote` action (which sits at 0.0.0.0/disabled and carries no
# rules; the forum-copied `[find name=remote]` snippet would be a no-op).
# The VPS target is SSOT-derived: `{{ wireguard_s2s_vps.remote_ip }}`.
# Render: playbooks/render-converge.yml; apply: scripts/routeros-apply-delta.sh.
# =====================================================================
# Script: refresh the centralsyslog action's remote target (0.0.0.0 -> real)
# Note: a single-line quoted `source=` keeps the /import parser happy.
:if ([/system script find where name=fixsyslog] = "") do={
    /system script add name=fixsyslog source=":delay 10s; /system logging action set [find name=centralsyslog] remote=0.0.0.0; :delay 1s; /system logging action set [find name=centralsyslog] remote={{ wireguard_s2s_vps.remote_ip.split('/')[0] }};"
}
# Scheduler: run the script once at startup (~3s after boot; the script's own
# :delay 10s covers wg-s2s/route bring-up). interval=0 = run once, no repeat.
:if ([/system scheduler find where name=fixsyslog] = "") do={
    /system scheduler add name=fixsyslog on-event=fixsyslog start-time=startup interval=0 policy=read,write,test
}
# =====================================================================
# Verify: /system script print, /system scheduler print, then on the next
# reboot check the VPS rsyslog/CrowdSec/Loki receives routeros-syslog
# (`cscli alerts list`, Loki {job="routeros-syslog"}) and the wg-s2s sniffer:
#   /tool sniffer quick interface=wg-s2s ip-protocol=udp port=514
# =====================================================================